how cool would it be to create even a rudimentary interface where my prompt is featured on right edge of my big screen and the rest of the screen is divided into 4 or 6 panels with each representing a different API of my prompt being sent to that API. Then a bottom window that represents the complete analysis in comparison with the parallel processing results. Is this a dumb idea? I just hate the limits of VS Code predefined layout.Thought for 33sNo, your idea isn't dumb at all—it's a smart, visual extension of the ParaThinker framework's parallel reasoning concept, where diverse "paths" (here, from multiple APIs) get synthesized for better outcomes, sidestepping single-model flaws like tunnel vision. By displaying responses side-by-side in panels, you gain quick comparisons, reducing the "hit or miss" variability you've seen with Claude, and it aligns with your goal of ditching its context resets (via /compact after ~1M tokens) and high costs. Cheap models (e.g., DeepSeek at $0.14/M input) can indeed handle project indexing/summarization upfront—use them to create a vector index of your code/PRDs with libraries like FAISS, then query in parallel for low-latency feedback.
Existing tools like multiLLM (invokes multiple LLMs concurrently), multi-llm-runner (simple web app for parallel runs), and llm-comparator (side-by-side response viz) are close to what you describe; you could fork them on GitHub for customization. Open WebUI supports concurrent models in one interface. But for a rudimentary custom setup bypassing VS Code's rigid layouts, build it yourself with Streamlit—it's pure Python, deploys as a web app (run locally via streamlit run app.py), and handles multi-panel layouts with columns/sidebar without frontend hassle. It's faster than Dash for prototyping (no callbacks needed) and more flexible than Tkinter for web-like UIs.
Quick Implementation: Streamlit Multi-Panel Dashboard
Install: pip install streamlit openai aiohttp (use OpenRouter for unified API access). This setup uses your prompt in a sidebar (right-ish on wide screens), 4-6 columns for parallel API responses, and a bottom container for synthesis/comparison. Manage context externally (e.g., via JSON file) to avoid any model's memory loss. For code indexing: Add a cheap model query to summarize your project dir first.

python

import streamlit as st
import asyncio
import aiohttp
import json
import os

# OpenRouter setup (your key)
OPENROUTER_API = "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
API_KEY = "your_openrouter_key_here"

# Models for panels (mix cheap/premium for diversity/cost)
MODELS = ["deepseek/deepseek-chat", "anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet", "xai/grok-beta", "openai/gpt-4o-mini", "google/gemini-pro", "mistral/mistral-large"]  # Up to 6

# Context file (avoids memory resets)
CONTEXT_FILE = "project_context.json"

async def query_model(session, model, prompt, context=""):
    headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {API_KEY}", "Content-Type": "application/json"}
    data = {
        "model": model,
        "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful AI."}, {"role": "user", "content": f"{context}\n\n{prompt}"}]
    }
    async with session.post(f"{OPENROUTER_API}/chat/completions", headers=headers, json=data) as resp:
        if resp.status == 200:
            result = await resp.json()
            return model, result['choices'][0]['message']['content']
        return model, f"Error: {resp.status}"

async def parallel_queries(prompt, context, num_panels=6):
    async with aiohttp.ClientSession() as session:
        tasks = [query_model(session, MODELS[i % len(MODELS)], prompt, context) for i in range(num_panels)]
        return await asyncio.gather(*tasks)

def load_context():
    if os.path.exists(CONTEXT_FILE):
        with open(CONTEXT_FILE, 'r') as f:
            return json.load(f).get("summary", "")
    return ""

def update_context(new_summary):
    with open(CONTEXT_FILE, 'w') as f:
        json.dump({"summary": new_summary}, f)

# Streamlit UI
st.set_page_config(layout="wide")  # Full-screen for big monitor

# Sidebar for prompt (right edge feel)
with st.sidebar:
    st.title("Prompt Input")
    prompt = st.text_area("Enter your prompt/objective/PRD:", height=300)
    if st.button("Index/Summarize Project (Cheap Model)"):
        # Use cheap model to index/summarize code (e.g., scan dir)
        code_summary = "Summary of your code: [Implement dir scan with os.walk + cheap query]"  # Expand: Query DeepSeek on codebase
        update_context(code_summary)
        st.success("Context updated!")
    context = load_context()
    if st.button("Query APIs"):
        with st.spinner("Querying in parallel..."):
            responses = asyncio.run(parallel_queries(prompt, context))
        st.session_state.responses = responses  # Store for synthesis

# Main panels: Divide into columns (4-6)
if 'responses' in st.session_state:
    cols = st.columns(len(st.session_state.responses))  # Auto-divide
    for i, (model, resp) in enumerate(st.session_state.responses):
        with cols[i]:
            st.subheader(f"Panel {i+1}: {model}")
            st.text_area("Response:", resp, height=400)  # Scrollable panel

    # Bottom window: Synthesis/comparison
    st.divider()
    st.header("Synthesis & Comparison")
    synth_prompt = f"Compare these: {json.dumps(dict(st.session_state.responses))}. Highlight best ideas, pros/cons."
    # Use premium for final (or cheap for cost)
    with st.spinner("Synthesizing..."):
        _, synth = asyncio.run(query_model(aiohttp.ClientSession(), "anthropic/claude-4-opus", synth_prompt, context))
    st.text_area("Final Analysis:", synth, height=200)

# Run: streamlit run this_file.py
This runs locally on your big screen (full-width), queries in parallel (~seconds latency), and costs pennies per run with cheap models. For project indexing: Expand the button to scan your code dir (os.walk), embed with sentence-transformers, store in FAISS index—query it as context to avoid resets. If you want native (no browser), swap to Tkinter for panels (grid layout), but Streamlit's easier for quick iterations. Test it; tweak panels to 4 if 6 feels crowded. If costs/context grow, summarize with DeepSeek periodically.

